export default {"id":"drums-level-7","number":7,"title":"Launchkey MK4 Performance","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the drums textbook","summary":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes The Novation Launchkey MK4 61 features a dedicated 2 × 8 grid of 16 velocity-sensitive Force-Sensing Resistor (FSR) pads equipped with per-pad polyphonic aftertouch. Mastering hardware configuration, pad","modules":[{"id":"drums-m7-1","code":"7.1","title":"Launchkey MK4 Performance — Modes, Notes & Curves","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes The Novation Launchkey MK4 61 features a dedicated 2 × 8 grid of 16 velocity-sensitive Force-Sensing Resistor (FSR) pads equipped with per-pad polyphonic aftertouch. Mastering hardware configuration, pad","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-7-1","title":"Section 7.1 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Novation Launchkey MK4 61</strong> features a dedicated 2 × 8 grid of 16 velocity-sensitive Force-Sensing Resistor (FSR) pads equipped with per-pad polyphonic aftertouch. Mastering hardware configuration, pad modes, MIDI channel routing, velocity transfer curves, and LED feedback states is necessary to transform the controller into a professional finger-drumming instrument."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Navigate</strong> the physical topology of the Launchkey MK4 16-pad array and Shift command layers.","<strong>Configure</strong> <strong>Drum Mode</strong> to transmit standard General MIDI percussion note numbers (<strong>36–51 / C1–D\\#2</strong>) on <strong>MIDI Channel 10</strong>.","<strong>Switch</strong> seamlessly between <strong>Drum Mode</strong>, <strong>Sequencer Mode</strong>, and <strong>Clip Launch Mode</strong>.","<strong>Calibrate</strong> hardware Velocity Transfer Curves (<strong>Soft</strong>, <strong>Normal</strong>, <strong>Hard</strong>, <strong>Fixed</strong>) and configure Fixed Velocity defaults.","<strong>Configure</strong> external visual feedback to mirror Ableton Live pad playback states onto hardware RGB LEDs."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-1","title":"7.1.1: Hardware Tour: 16 FSR Pads","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.1: Hardware Tour: 16 FSR Pads","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Unlike 4 × 4 square pad controllers (e.g., MPC, Maschine), the Launchkey MK4 features a wide <strong>2 × 8 rectangular pad array</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                              NOVATION LAUNCHKEY MK4 61 PAD ARRAY                                 |\n|                                                                                                  |\n|  TOP ROW (Pads 9-16):                                                                            |\n|  [ Pad 9: G#1 ] [ Pad 10: A1 ] [ Pad 11: A#1] [ Pad 12: B1 ] [ Pad 13: C2 ] ... [ Pad 16: D#2 ]|\n|  (Note 44)      (Note 45)      (Note 46)      (Note 47)      (Note 48)          (Note 51)       |\n|                                                                                                  |\n|  BOTTOM ROW (Pads 1-8):                                                                          |\n|  [ Pad 1: C1  ] [ Pad 2: C#1 ] [ Pad 3: D1  ] [ Pad 4: D#1 ] [ Pad 5: E1  ] ... [ Pad 8: G1  ] |\n|  (Note 36)      (Note 37)      (Note 38)      (Note 39)      (Note 40)          (Note 43)       |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Physical Mechanics:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Force-Sensing Resistor (FSR) Technology:</strong> Striking a pad compresses conductive elastomer layers, instantly decreasing electrical resistance to measure velocity and continuous pressure.","<strong>RGB Backlighting:</strong> Each pad contains full RGB LEDs capable of displaying velocity intensity, clip playback status, and mute/solo assignments directly from Live 12."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-2","title":"7.1.2: Drum Mode: Notes 36–51 on MIDI Channel 10","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.2: Drum Mode: Notes 36–51 on MIDI Channel 10","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In <strong>Drum Mode</strong>, the Launchkey MK4 bypasses the keyboard's active octave and scale locks, anchoring its 16 pads strictly to standard General MIDI drum note numbers:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 LAUNCHKEY MK4 DRUM MODE MIDI NOTE &amp; OCTAVE MAP\n==================================================================================================\nPAD NUMBER   PHYSICAL POSITION     MIDI NOTE NAME   MIDI NOTE NO.   STANDARD GM VOICE\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPad 1        Bottom-Left (Corner)  C1               36              Bass Drum / Kick 1\nPad 2        Bottom Row - Slot 2   C#1              37              Side Stick / Rimshot\nPad 3        Bottom Row - Slot 3   D1               38              Acoustic Snare\nPad 4        Bottom Row - Slot 4   D#1              39              Hand Clap\nPad 5        Bottom Row - Slot 5   E1               40              Electric Snare\nPad 6        Bottom Row - Slot 6   F1               41              Low Floor Tom\nPad 7        Bottom Row - Slot 7   F#1              42              Closed Hi-Hat\nPad 8        Bottom Row - Slot 8   G1               43              High Floor Tom\nPad 9        Top-Left (Corner)     G#1              44              Pedal Hi-Hat\nPad 10       Top Row - Slot 2      A1               45              Low Tom\nPad 11       Top Row - Slot 3      A#1              46              Open Hi-Hat\nPad 12       Top Row - Slot 4      B1               47              Low-Mid Tom\nPad 13       Top Row - Slot 5      C2               48              High-Mid Tom\nPad 14       Top Row - Slot 6      C#2              49              Crash Cymbal 1\nPad 15       Top Row - Slot 7      D2               50              High Tom\nPad 16       Top-Right (Corner)    D#2              51              Ride Cymbal 1\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-3","title":"7.1.3: Sequencer Mode: 16 Steps for Selected Drum Pad","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.3: Sequencer Mode: 16 Steps for Selected Drum Pad","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pressing <code>Shift + Pad 2 (Sequencer)</code> transforms the 2 × 8 pad array into a hardware <strong>16-Step Drum Sequencer</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                           16-STEP HARDWARE SEQUENCER TOPOLOGY\n==================================================================================================\nTOP ROW:     [ Step 09 ] [ Step 10 ] [ Step 11 ] [ Step 12 ] [ Step 13 ] [ Step 14 ] [ Step 15 ] [ Step 16 ]\n             (Beat 3.1)  (Beat 3.2)  (Beat 3.3)  (Beat 3.4)  (Beat 4.1)  (Beat 4.2)  (Beat 4.3)  (Beat 4.4)\nBOTTOM ROW:  [ Step 01 ] [ Step 02 ] [ Step 03 ] [ Step 04 ] [ Step 05 ] [ Step 06 ] [ Step 07 ] [ Step 08 ]\n             (Beat 1.1)  (Beat 1.2)  (Beat 1.3)  (Beat 1.4)  (Beat 2.1)  (Beat 2.2)  (Beat 2.3)  (Beat 2.4)\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Operation:</strong> Select any drum pad in Drum Rack (e.g., Closed Hat on F\\#1). Switch to Sequencer Mode. Tapping pads toggles active 16th-note step triggers directly inside the active Live MIDI clip."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-4","title":"7.1.4: Clip Mode vs. Drum Mode","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.4: Clip Mode vs. Drum Mode","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  CLIP MODE vs. DRUM MODE DISTINCTION                    |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ CLIP MODE (Shift + Pad 1) ]                                          |\n|  • Pads launch Session View clips and scenes                            |\n|  • Pads light up matching Session clip colors                           |\n|  • Striking pads does NOT trigger Drum Rack samples                     |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ DRUM MODE (Shift + Pad 3) ]                                          |\n|  • Pads transmit Notes 36-51 on Channel 10                              |\n|  • Striking pads triggers Drum Rack instruments directly                |\n|  • Hardware OLED displays pad hit velocity                              |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-5","title":"7.1.5: Pad Velocity Curves: Soft / Normal / Hard / Fixed","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.5: Pad Velocity Curves: Soft / Normal / Hard / Fixed","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Accessed via <strong>Settings → Pad Velocity Curve</strong> on the Launchkey MK4 OLED screen:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Strike Force (g) ] ---&gt; Transfer Function ---&gt; [ MIDI Velocity Output (0-127) ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                    LAUNCHKEY MK4 PAD VELOCITY CURVE SPECIFICATIONS\n==================================================================================================\nCURVE SETTING    TRANSFER FUNCTION DYNAMICS           BEST PRODUCTION USE\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSoft             High output at low physical force    Fast 16th/32nd-note rolls; prevents fatigue\nNormal (Default) Linear 1:1 dynamic response          Standard finger drumming; balanced range\nHard             Requires heavy physical force for 127 High dynamic control; prevents accidental accents\nFixed            Transmits static locked velocity     Step sequencing; electronic 4/4 beats; house kicks\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-6","title":"7.1.6: Fixed Velocity Mode","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.6: Fixed Velocity Mode","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Engaging Fixed Velocity:</strong> Press the dedicated <code>Fixed Velocity</code> button on the Launchkey chassis.","<strong>Default Value:</strong> Out-of-the-box default is <strong>Velocity 120</strong>.","<strong>Adjusting Fixed Value:</strong> In <strong>Settings → Fixed Velocity Value</strong>, dial any integer between 1 and 127.","<em>When to use:</em> Perfect for programming consistent, maximum-impact kicks and snares without velocity fluctuation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-7","title":"7.1.7: Drums Channel Setting","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.7: Drums Channel Setting","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In <strong>Settings → Drums Channel</strong>, verify that the target MIDI output channel is set to <strong>Channel 10</strong> (standard General MIDI drum channel).","This ensures your drum pad performances never interfere with melodic instruments receiving on MIDI Channels 1–9."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-8","title":"7.1.8: External Feedback: Pads Light from Live","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.8: External Feedback: Pads Light from Live","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 supports <strong>External MIDI Feedback</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["When a MIDI clip plays back in Ableton Live, incoming MIDI note-on events stream back to the controller, illuminating the corresponding RGB pads in real time.","In <strong>Settings → External Feedback</strong>, ensure this is set to <strong>On</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-1-9","title":"7.1.9: Power-On Pad Mode Configuration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.1.9: Power-On Pad Mode Configuration","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To prevent the controller from powering on in Clip Launch Mode:"},{"type":"list","items":["Enter the hardware <strong>Settings</strong> menu.","Set <strong>Default Pad Mode</strong> to <code>Drum</code>.","The controller will now immediately boot into Drum Mode upon USB power connection."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 7.1: Hardware Configuration Verification","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect the Launchkey MK4 61 via USB to your Windows 11 PC.","On the controller, navigate to <strong>Settings</strong>:","Set <strong>Pad Velocity Curve</strong> to <code>Normal</code>.","Verify <strong>Drums Channel</strong> is set to <code>10</code>.","Set <strong>Default Pad Mode</strong> to <code>Drum</code>.","Open Ableton Live 12. Load an empty Drum Rack onto Track 1.","Strike Pad 1 (bottom-left) and confirm that Pad C1 triggers in Live. Strike Pad 16 (top-right) and confirm that Pad D#2 triggers.","Toggle <code>Fixed Velocity</code> and confirm that all hits register at exactly Velocity 120."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What is the standard MIDI note number and note name for Pad 1 (bottom-left) in Launchkey MK4 Drum Mode?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> MIDI Note 36 (C1).","<strong>What MIDI Channel does the Launchkey MK4 transmit on when operating in Drum Mode?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>MIDI Channel 10</strong>.","<strong>How do you switch the Launchkey MK4 pads into the 16-step hardware step sequencer?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Press <code>Shift + Pad 2 (Sequencer)</code>."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m7-2","code":"7.2","title":"Launchkey MK4 Performance — Ergonomic Finger Drumming Technique","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Finger drumming on Force-Sensing Resistor (FSR) pads is an athletic physical discipline requiring biomechanical efficiency, relaxed wrist posture, and digit coordination. Applying incorrect technique cau","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-7-2","title":"Section 7.2 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Finger drumming on Force-Sensing Resistor (FSR) pads is an athletic physical discipline requiring biomechanical efficiency, relaxed wrist posture, and digit coordination. Applying incorrect technique causes physical fatigue, tendon strain, inconsistent velocity response, and timing jitter. Grounded in foundational methodologies (<em>Melodics Finger Drumming Guide</em>, <em>Unison Finger Drumming 101</em>, and <em>eMastered Complete Guide</em>), this module establishes professional ergonomics tailored specifically to the Launchkey MK4's 2 × 8 pad topology."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Adopt</strong> the professional elevated-wrist ergonomic posture, eliminating chassis drag.","<strong>Execute</strong> elastic rebound strikes using curved fingertips.","<strong>Deploy</strong> two-finger alternation (Index + Middle) to execute rapid 16th-note streams at 174 BPM.","<strong>Map</strong> a custom 2 × 8 drum layout that places primary anchors (Kick and Snare) under dominant digits.","<strong>Calibrate</strong> hardware pad sensitivity and aftertouch thresholds to match personal striking force.","<strong>Implement</strong> the 5-minute/1-minute fatigue mitigation protocol."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-1","title":"7.2.1: Wrists Elevated: Zero Chassis Resting","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.1: Wrists Elevated: Zero Chassis Resting","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The most severe ergonomic error in finger drumming is resting the palms or wrists on the controller chassis:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  INCORRECT POSTURE vs. CORRECT POSTURE                  |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ INCORRECT: ANCHORED WRISTS ]                                         |\n|  • Wrists resting on controller desk/chassis                            |\n|  • Compresses carpal tunnel; limits finger range of motion              |\n|  • Forces strikes to originate purely from small finger tendons         |\n|  • Result: Rapid tendon fatigue, weak velocity, high injury risk        |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ CORRECT: ELEVATED SUSPENDED WRISTS ]                                 |\n|  • Wrists held 2-3 cm above the chassis in a neutral arch               |\n|  • Forearm, wrist, and hand form a unified kinetic lever                |\n|  • Striking power originates from forearm weight, not tendon strain     |\n|  • Result: Maximum dynamic velocity range, effortless endurance         |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-2","title":"7.2.2: Curved Fingers and Fingertip Striking","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.2: Curved Fingers and Fingertip Striking","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Hand Shape:</strong> Form a relaxed, natural dome as if holding a tennis ball.","<strong>Contact Point:</strong> Strike the center of the pad using the fleshy pad immediately adjacent to the fingertip.","<strong>Nail Maintenance:</strong> Keep fingernails trimmed short. Long nails strike the rubber pad before the fingertip, absorbing kinetic energy and preventing the FSR sensor from measuring full velocity."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-3","title":"7.2.3: Elastic Rebound: Bounce Off the Pad","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.3: Elastic Rebound: Bounce Off the Pad","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Never press into an FSR pad like clicking a computer mouse. Finger drumming requires <strong>Elastic Rebound</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Downward Strike Vector ] ---&gt; Contact Pad Surface (&lt; 5 ms) ---&gt; [ Immediate Upward Snap ]\n                                                                          |\n                                                                Pad Vibrates Freely;\n                                                                Zero Dampening Force</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Kinetic Principle:","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The finger must bounce off the pad surface instantaneously upon impact. Lingering on the pad dampens physical resonance, triggers accidental aftertouch messages, and prevents rapid subsequent strikes."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-4","title":"7.2.4: Finger + Wrist Coordination","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.4: Finger + Wrist Coordination","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Striking force is distributed across three mechanical joints:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Forearm / Wrist:</strong> Provides the macro-weight and velocity ceiling (100–127).","<strong>The Knuckle Joint (MCP):</strong> Provides metric agility and subdivision speed.","<strong>The Fingertip Joint (DIP):</strong> Remains firm to transmit kinetic energy directly into the sensor."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-5","title":"7.2.5: Forefinger Linear Technique","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.5: Forefinger Linear Technique","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"For single-line rhythmic motifs (e.g., programming a 4-on-the-floor kick or sparse dubstep snare), the dominant index finger executes linear down-strokes with piston-like precision."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-6","title":"7.2.6: Two-Finger Alternation for 16ths at 174 BPM","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.6: Two-Finger Alternation for 16ths at 174 BPM","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"At 174 BPM, a single finger cannot physically articulate continuous 16th notes (86.2 ms intervals) without severe muscle tension. <strong>Two-Finger Alternation (Index + Middle)</strong> is mandatory:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 TWO-FINGER ALTERNATION ON CLOSED HI-HAT (PAD 7 / F#1)\n==================================================================================================\nMETRIC STEP:    01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nDIGIT (RIGHT): [MID] [IND] [MID] [IND] [MID] [IND] [MID] [IND] [MID] [IND] [MID] [IND] [MID] [IND] [MID] [IND]\nVELOCITY:       115   60    95    55   118   65    98    58   115   60    95    55   120   68   100    62\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Biomechanics:","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By alternating the Middle finger (stronger downbeat) and Index finger (softer upbeat), the physical repetition rate per finger is cut in half (172.4 ms per digit), allowing effortless execution at 174 BPM."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-7","title":"7.2.7: Full-Hand Layout on 2×8 Topology","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.7: Full-Hand Layout on 2×8 Topology","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4's 2 × 8 layout requires a horizontal ergonomics strategy:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                            OPTIMIZED 2x8 ERGONOMIC PAD LAYOUT                                    |\n|                                                                                                  |\n|  LEFT HAND ZONE (Tops &amp; Percussion)              RIGHT HAND ZONE (Core Foundation)               |\n|  [ Pad 9: PH ] [ Pad 10: T1 ] [ Pad 11: OH ] [ Pad 12: CR ] | [ Pad 13: RD ] [ Pad 14: T2 ] [ Pad 15: T3 ] [ Pad 16: FX ]|\n|                                                              |                                   |\n|  [ Pad 1: KICK] [ Pad 2: RIM] [ Pad 3: SNARE] [ Pad 4: CLAP]| [ Pad 5: G-SN] [ Pad 6: PERC] [ Pad 7: CH ] [ Pad 8: SHK ]|\n|    ^ Left Thumb   ^ Left Index  ^ Left Middle   ^ Left Ring  |   ^ R-Thumb     ^ R-Index     ^ R-Middle    ^ R-Ring |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-8","title":"7.2.8: Why 8×8 and 4×4 Layouts Fail on 2×8 Hardware","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.8: Why 8×8 and 4×4 Layouts Fail on 2×8 Hardware","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Many online finger-drumming courses teach classic 4 × 4 MPC square layouts (Kick on Pad 1 bottom-left, Snare on Pad 2, Hats on Pad 3 above). Attempting to map 4 × 4 vertical coordinates onto a 2 × 8 horizontal controller forces awkward hand crossover and wrist twisting. <strong>Always use horizontal split-hand mapping on the Launchkey MK4.</strong>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-9","title":"7.2.9: Sensitivity & Aftertouch Threshold Calibration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.9: Sensitivity & Aftertouch Threshold Calibration","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Launchkey MK4 <strong>Settings</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["If your natural strikes struggle to reach velocity 127, switch <strong>Pad Velocity Curve</strong> to <code>Soft</code>.","Set <strong>Pad AT Threshold</strong> to <code>Normal</code> (or <code>High</code>) to prevent hard strikes from accidentally triggering unwanted aftertouch modulation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-2-10","title":"7.2.10: Fatigue Prevention: The 5-on-1-off Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.2.10: Fatigue Prevention: The 5-on-1-off Protocol","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  FATIGUE &amp; INJURY PREVENTION PROTOCOL                   |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • 5 Minutes Active Pad Practice ---&gt; Focus on loose, elastic rebound   |\n|  • 1 Minute Complete Rest         ---&gt; Drop arms to sides; shake wrists |\n|  • Warm-up: 60 seconds slow finger stretches before high-BPM practice   |\n|  • Immediate Stop: If any burning sensation occurs in wrists/forearms   |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 7.2: Alternation & Ergonomics Workshop","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Configure your Launchkey MK4 in <strong>Drum Mode</strong> with <strong>Pad Velocity Curve: Normal</strong>.","Set Ableton Live project tempo to <code>140.00 BPM</code>.","Practice the <strong>Two-Finger Alternation Technique</strong> on Pad 7 (Closed Hat):","Middle finger on all downbeats (Steps 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15).","Index finger on all upbeats (Steps 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16).","Increase tempo to <code>174.00 BPM</code>. Maintain loose, elevated wrists for 3 minutes.","Record a pass in Live. Inspect the MIDI velocity lane to confirm clean dynamic wave alternation."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Why must wrists be held elevated in a neutral arch rather than resting on the controller chassis?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Resting wrists compresses the carpal tunnel, restricts tendon mobility, and forces fingers to generate strike force without the kinetic assistance of forearm mass.","<strong>What technique enables continuous 16th-note hi-hat execution at 174 BPM without muscle tension?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>Two-Finger Alternation</strong> (alternating Middle and Index fingers on the same pad).","<strong>What is Elastic Rebound in finger drumming?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> The technique of allowing the striking finger to bounce off the rubber pad surface immediately upon impact rather than pressing or resting on the pad."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m7-3","code":"7.3","title":"Launchkey MK4 Performance — Rudiments & Coordination on 16 Pads","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Acoustic drum rudiments—the foundational patterns of physical drumming—translate directly to finger drumming on the Launchkey MK4. Developing bilateral digit independence allows the producer to perform c","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-7-3","title":"Section 7.3 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Acoustic drum rudiments—the foundational patterns of physical drumming—translate directly to finger drumming on the Launchkey MK4. Developing bilateral digit independence allows the producer to perform complex, syncopated rhythms, ghost-note fills, and genre-specific backbeats live without post-quantization. Grounded in <em>Unison Finger Drumming 101</em> and <em>Melodics Rudiments Training</em>, this module adapts single strokes, double strokes, paradiddles, and flams to the 2 × 8 pad matrix."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Execute</strong> Single Stroke rolls, Double Stroke rolls, and Paradiddles across discrete pads with bilateral digit balance.","<strong>Perform</strong> Flam grace notes to add acoustic weight to snare backbeats.","<strong>Demonstrate</strong> digit independence by executing steady 16th-note hi-hat patterns while independently triggering syncopated kicks and snares.","<strong>Perform</strong> authentic real-time passes of House (4/4), Dubstep (1+3 Half-time), and Drum &amp; Bass (174 Linear Backbeat) patterns from the Launchkey MK4 pads."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-3-1","title":"7.3.1: The Single Stroke Roll","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.1: The Single Stroke Roll","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The foundational rudiment of all drumming:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Pattern:      | R   L   R   L   | R   L   R   L   | R   L   R   L   | R   L   R   L   |\nGrid (16ths): | 1   e   &amp;   a   | 2   e   &amp;   a   | 3   e   &amp;   a   | 4   e   &amp;   a   |\nRight Hand:   | Pad 7 (CH)      | Pad 7 (CH)      | Pad 7 (CH)      | Pad 7 (CH)      |\nLeft Hand:    | Pad 7 (CH)      | Pad 7 (CH)      | Pad 7 (CH)      | Pad 7 (CH)      |</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Target:</strong> Maintain strict velocity balance (± 5 MIDI velocity delta between Right and Left hands).","<strong>Tempo Ladder:</strong> Practice at 100 BPM → 140 BPM → 174 BPM."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-3-2","title":"7.3.2: The Double Stroke Roll","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.2: The Double Stroke Roll","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Pattern:      | R   R   L   L   | R   R   L   L   | R   R   L   L   | R   R   L   L   |\nGrid (16ths): | 1   e   &amp;   a   | 2   e   &amp;   a   | 3   e   &amp;   a   | 4   e   &amp;   a   |</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mechanics:</strong> Each hand executes two rapid rebound taps. Crucial for fast tom fills, 32nd-note snare rolls, and trap hi-hat ratchets."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-3-3","title":"7.3.3: The Paradiddle as Digit Independence","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.3: The Paradiddle as Digit Independence","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Paradiddle</strong> combines single and double strokes to naturally shift primary accents between hands:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 THE 16-STEP PARADIDDLE ON LAUNCHKEY MK4 PADS\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nSTICKING:      [R]   [L]   [R]   [R]   [L]   [R]   [L]   [L]   [R]   [L]   [R]   [R]   [L]   [R]   [L]   [L]\nACCENT:        [&gt;]    .     .     .    [&gt;]    .     .     .    [&gt;]    .     .     .    [&gt;]    .     .     .\nPAD MAP:       [SN]  [CH]  [CH]  [CH]  [KIK] [CH]  [CH]  [CH]  [SN]  [CH]  [CH]  [CH]  [KIK] [CH]  [CH]  [CH]\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Coordination Breakthrough:","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Notice how the Paradiddle places the primary accent on alternating hands (Right hand on Beat 1 &amp; 3; Left hand on Beat 2 &amp; 4), training complete bilateral limb independence."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-3-4","title":"7.3.4: Flams / Grace Notes for Massive Snares","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.4: Flams / Grace Notes for Massive Snares","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Flam</strong> consists of a low-velocity \"grace note\" striking slightly ahead of the primary backbeat:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Time:          0.0 ms                        +12.0 ms\nStrike Event: [ Left Index: Grace Hit ] ---&gt; [ Right Middle: Primary Snare ]\nVelocity:     [ Velocity: 45 ]               [ Velocity: 122 ]\nAcoustic:     Emulates two drumsticks striking a snare skin milliseconds apart.</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Execution:</strong> Drop both fingers toward Pad 3 (Snare D1) simultaneously, but release the left finger slightly lower so it strikes the pad 10–15 ms before the right finger."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-3-5","title":"7.3.5: 16th Hats: 140 vs. 174 Execution","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.5: 16th Hats: 140 vs. 174 Execution","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>At 140 BPM (107.1 ms):</strong> Single-hand two-finger alternation (Right Hand: Middle + Index) provides steady, relaxed execution.","<strong>At 174 BPM (86.2 ms):</strong> Two-hand single strokes (R-L-R-L) or rapid double strokes are used for high-velocity endurance."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-3-6","title":"7.3.6: Digit Independence: Constant Tops + Broken Foundation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.6: Digit Independence: Constant Tops + Broken Foundation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  DIGIT INDEPENDENCE DRILL (DUBSTEP 140)                 |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ RIGHT HAND (Steady Stream) ] ---&gt; Continuous 8th-note Closed Hats    |\n|                                      Pad 7 (F#1) on Steps 1, 3, 5, 7... |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ LEFT HAND (Broken Accents) ] ---&gt; Independent Kick &amp; Snare           |\n|                                      Pad 1 (Kick C1) on Step 1          |\n|                                      Pad 3 (Snare D1) on Step 9         |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-3-8","title":"7.3.8 – 7.3.10: Performing the Three Core Genre Skeletons","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.3.8 – 7.3.10: Performing the Three Core Genre Skeletons","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                        GENRE PERFORMANCE PROTOCOLS (LAUNCHKEY MK4)\n==================================================================================================\nGENRE                 TEMPO       LEFT HAND (FOUNDATION)           RIGHT HAND (TOPS)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. House (Lesson 7.2.8) 125 BPM   Pad 1 (Kick): Steps 1, 5, 9, 13  Pad 11 (OH): Steps 3, 7, 11, 15\n                                  Pad 4 (Clap): Steps 5, 13        Pad 7 (CH): Continuous 16ths\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2. Dubstep (7.2.9)    140 BPM     Pad 1 (Kick): Step 1             Pad 7 (CH): Steps 1, 5, 9, 13\n                                  Pad 3 (Snare): Step 9            Pad 11 (OH): Bar 2 Step 15\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3. DnB (7.2.10)       174 BPM     Pad 1 (Kick): Steps 1, 11        Pad 7 (CH): Two-Finger Alternation\n                                  Pad 3 (Snare): Steps 5, 13       Pad 5 (Ghost Snare): Steps 8, 10\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 7.3: Live Performance Assessment","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Live tempo to <code>140.00 BPM</code>.","Arm the Drum Rack track for recording.","Perform a continuous 4-bar Dubstep pass:","Kick on 1, Snare on 9.","Continuous 8th-note hi-hats.","Execute a Flam on the Bar 4 Step 9 snare.","Set tempo to <code>174.00 BPM</code>.","Perform a continuous 4-bar Drum &amp; Bass pass (Kick on 1 &amp; 11, Snare on 5 &amp; 13) using two-finger alternation on hats.","Verify timing accuracy in Clip View."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What is the sticking formula for a standard 16-step single Paradiddle?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <code>R - L - R - R - L - R - L - L</code>.","<strong>How does executing a Flam grace note improve electronic snare realism?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> It emulates the physical acoustic reality of two drumsticks striking a drumhead with a micro-millisecond timing offset, expanding the perceived transient width and acoustic size of the snare.","<strong>What are the exact pad strike coordinates for performing a 174 BPM DnB foundation?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Kick on Pad 1 (Steps 1 and 11); Snare on Pad 3 (Steps 5 and 13); Closed Hat on Pad 7 (Continuous 16ths)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m7-4","code":"7.4","title":"Launchkey MK4 Performance — Polyphonic Aftertouch vs. MPE Routing","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes A major area of technical confusion in modern MIDI hardware is the distinction between Channel Aftertouch, Polyphonic Aftertouch, and MIDI Polyphonic Expression (MPE). The Novation Launchkey MK4 features","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-7-4","title":"Section 7.4 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A major area of technical confusion in modern MIDI hardware is the distinction between <strong>Channel Aftertouch</strong>, <strong>Polyphonic Aftertouch</strong>, and <strong>MIDI Polyphonic Expression (MPE)</strong>. The Novation Launchkey MK4 features <strong>per-pad Polyphonic Aftertouch</strong> on its 16 FSR pads, while its 61-key keyboard bed contains <em>no aftertouch</em>. Furthermore, incorrectly enabling Ableton Live's \"MPE\" checkbox on the Launchkey MIDI input port monopolizes MIDI channels and breaks standard Channel-10 Drum Rack routing."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Differentiate</strong> Channel Aftertouch, Polyphonic Aftertouch, and MPE at the MIDI byte and protocol level.","<strong>Configure</strong> Launchkey MK4 hardware aftertouch settings (<strong>Off</strong>, <strong>Channel</strong>, <strong>Poly</strong>) and calibrate pressure thresholds (<strong>Low</strong>, <strong>Normal</strong>, <strong>High</strong>).","<strong>Diagnose</strong> and eliminate the MIDI Channel-10 Drum Rack breakdown caused by enabling Live's MPE port checkbox.","<strong>Map</strong> per-pad polyphonic aftertouch to modulate hi-hat decay in Drum Sampler and filter cutoff in DS HH via <strong>Expression Control</strong>.","<strong>Acknowledge</strong> the physical hardware specification: aftertouch resides on the 16 pads, <em>not</em> on the 61-key keyboard bed.","<strong>Pass</strong> Gate Assessment D."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-4-1","title":"7.4.1 & 7.4.2: Channel Aftertouch vs. Polyphonic Aftertouch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.1 & 7.4.2: Channel Aftertouch vs. Polyphonic Aftertouch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  CHANNEL AFTERTOUCH vs. POLYPHONIC AFTERTOUCH           |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ CHANNEL AFTERTOUCH (Status Byte 0xD0) ]                              |\n|  • Single global pressure stream transmitted on MIDI Channel 10         |\n|  • Pressing Pad 1 sends pressure; ALL 16 pads are affected equally      |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ POLYPHONIC AFTERTOUCH (Status Byte 0xA0) ]                           |\n|  • Discrete per-note pressure stream [ Note Number + Pressure Value ]   |\n|  • Pressing Pad 6 (Hat) modulates hat decay; Pad 1 (Kick) is UNAFFECTED |\n|  • THIS IS THE NATIVE HARDWARE SPECIFICATION OF THE LAUNCHKEY MK4 PADS |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-4-5","title":"7.4.5 & 7.4.6: Why the Launchkey MK4 is Polyphonic Aftertouch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.5 & 7.4.6: Why the Launchkey MK4 is Polyphonic Aftertouch","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Polyphonic Aftertouch:</strong> Operates on a <strong>single MIDI channel</strong> (Channel 10). It sends Note-On, Note-Off, and discrete per-note Poly Pressure messages (0–127).","<strong>MPE (MIDI Polyphonic Expression):</strong> Requires splitting polyphonic performance across <strong>15 discrete MIDI channels</strong> (Channels 2–16) with per-note Pitch Bend, Channel Pressure, and CC74 (Slide/Timbre).","<strong>Official Specification:</strong> Novation explicitly designed the Launchkey MK4 pads with <strong>Polyphonic Aftertouch</strong>. It is <em>not</em> an MPE multidimensional controller (such as a Roli Seaboard or LinnStrument)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-4-3","title":"7.4.3 & 7.4.4: Hardware Settings & Pressure Thresholds","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.3 & 7.4.4: Hardware Settings & Pressure Thresholds","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On the Launchkey MK4 OLED screen, navigate to <strong>Settings</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 LAUNCHKEY MK4 AFTERTOUCH HARDWARE CONFIGURATION\n==================================================================================================\nMENU ITEM             OPTIONS                   PRODUCTION STANDARD\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPad Aftertouch        [ Off | Channel | Poly ]  Set to [ POLYPHONIC ] (Default)\nPad AT Threshold      [ Low | Normal | High ]   Set to [ NORMAL ] or [ HIGH ]\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Threshold Calibration:</strong> Setting AT Threshold to <code>High</code> requires firm deliberate pressure after the initial strike, ensuring fast finger drumming does not accidentally transmit unwanted pressure modulation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-4-7","title":"7.4.7: The Live MPE Checkbox Trap","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.7: The Live MPE Checkbox Trap","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                      THE CRITICAL MIDI PORT SETTING                     |\n|                                                                         |\n|  In Ableton Live 12: Preferences -&gt; MIDI Ports                          |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ In: Launchkey MK4 (MIDI) ]                                           |\n|  • Track     : [ ON ]                                                   |\n|  • Sync      : [ OFF ]                                                  |\n|  • Remote    : [ ON ]                                                   |\n|  • MPE       : [ MUST BE OFF FOR DRUM PRODUCTION! ]                     |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Technical Mechanism of the Failure:","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the <strong>MPE checkbox</strong> is enabled on the Launchkey MIDI input port in Live Preferences, Live's MIDI engine intercepts all incoming channel data and dynamically re-routes notes across Channels 2–16. This breaks Drum Racks set to listen specifically on <strong>Channel 10</strong>, causing dropped notes, stuck voices, and erratic pad triggering. <strong>Keep MPE disabled for this course.</strong>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-4-11","title":"7.4.11: Expression Control: The Native Modulation Bridge","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.11: Expression Control: The Native Modulation Bridge","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To route Polyphonic Aftertouch to any device parameter inside a Drum Rack in Ableton Live 12 Standard, insert the stock <strong>Expression Control</strong> MIDI device:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Launchkey Pad (Pad F#1 / Closed Hat) ] ---&gt; Transmits Poly Aftertouch (Note 42)\n                                                      |\n                                                      v\n[ Drum Rack Chain (F#1) ] ---&gt; [ Expression Control Device ]\n                                      |\n                                      +--&gt; Source: [ Pressure (Poly Aftertouch) ]\n                                      +--&gt; Map To: [ Drum Sampler Decay / DS HH Tone ]\n                                      +--&gt; Min: 35 ms | Max: 450 ms</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-4-9","title":"7.4.9 & 7.4.10: Applied Modulation Recipes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Modulating Hi-Hat Decay via Pad Pressure:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Map Expression Control <code>Pressure</code> to <strong>Drum Sampler Decay</strong>.","Light finger tap = Short dry closed tick (35 ms).","Pressing deeper into Pad 7 (F\\#1) = Opens the hi-hat decay continuously up to 450 ms, creating an expressive semi-open hat on demand."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Modulating DS HH Filter Cutoff via Pressure:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Map Expression Control <code>Pressure</code> to <strong>DS HH Tone</strong>.","Pressing Pad 7 sweeps the filter cutoff from 8 kHz to 16 kHz, adding dynamic sizzle during live performance."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-4-12","title":"7.4.12: Hardware Reality: Keys Have NO Aftertouch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.4.12: Hardware Reality: Keys Have NO Aftertouch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Explicit Studio Fact:</em> The Launchkey MK4 61 semi-weighted keyboard bed does <strong>not</strong> contain aftertouch sensors. Aftertouch is physically located <strong>only on the 16 FSR pads</strong>. Do not attempt to modulate synth parameters by pressing into the piano keys."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 7.4: Gate D Polyphonic Aftertouch Workshop","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Verify in <strong>Live Preferences → MIDI</strong> that the <code>MPE</code> checkbox on the Launchkey input port is <strong>Disabled (Off)</strong>.","In Launchkey <strong>Settings</strong>, verify <strong>Pad Aftertouch</strong> is set to <code>Poly</code>.","Create a Drum Rack with <strong>Drum Sampler</strong> on Pad F#1.","Insert <strong>Expression Control</strong> on the F#1 chain. Map <code>Pressure</code> to Drum Sampler <code>Decay</code> (Min: 35 ms, Max: 500 ms).","Strike Pad 7 lightly → hear short closed hat. Press firmly into the pad → hear the decay expand into an open hat.","Program a 2-bar pattern in Drum Mode, then switch to Sequencer Mode (<code>Shift + Pad 2</code>) and sequence a snare roll."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Gate Assessment D Checklist (drums-D)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To clear Gate D, verify the following self-assessment criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] You can play a live drum pattern in Drum Mode and edit steps in Sequencer Mode.","[ ] Polyphonic Aftertouch is mapped to modulate a drum parameter (e.g., decay or filter).","[ ] Ableton Live MIDI Preferences confirmed: MPE checkbox is <strong>OFF</strong> on the Launchkey port.","[ ] You can state without hesitation that aftertouch exists on the pads, not the keys."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Why does enabling the MPE checkbox on the Launchkey MK4 MIDI port break Drum Rack performance?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Live's MPE engine intercepts and dynamically redistributes incoming MIDI events across Channels 2–16, breaking standard Channel-10 Drum Rack routing and causing dropped hits.","<strong>What MIDI status byte class represents Polyphonic Aftertouch?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <code>0xA0</code> (Polyphonic Key Pressure: Note Number + Pressure Value).","<strong>Do the 61 piano keys on the Launchkey MK4 transmit aftertouch?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> No. Aftertouch sensors are physically implemented exclusively on the 16 FSR drum pads."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":20}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m7-5","code":"7.5","title":"Launchkey MK4 Performance — Hardware Recording & Step Sequencing","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Capturing a live drum performance requires a structured recording methodology. Attempting to record all 14 drum voices simultaneously in a single take often results in timing jitter, velocity inconsisten","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-7-5","title":"Section 7.5 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Capturing a live drum performance requires a structured recording methodology. Attempting to record all 14 drum voices simultaneously in a single take often results in timing jitter, velocity inconsistencies, and performance errors. This module details the <strong>Layer-by-Layer Overdub Protocol</strong>, hardware step sequencing via the Launchkey MK4, input quantization strategies, and Live 12's <strong>Capture MIDI</strong> workflow."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Execute</strong> professional multi-pass drum tracking using Live's MIDI Overdub (<code>+</code>) engine.","<strong>Configure</strong> Record Quantization (1/16, 1/8, Off) to balance human groove against metric precision.","<strong>Sequence</strong> drum patterns directly from the Launchkey MK4 hardware pads in Sequencer Mode.","<strong>Retrieve</strong> unrecorded rehearsal takes using Live's <strong>Capture MIDI</strong> function.","<strong>Manage</strong> non-destructive clip versioning during tracking sessions."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-5-1","title":"7.5.1: Track Arming, Count-In, and Pre-Roll","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.1: Track Arming, Count-In, and Pre-Roll","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before recording:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Track Arm:</strong> Click the <strong>Arm Recording</strong> button on the target Drum Rack MIDI track (or press <code>Track Select</code> on the Launchkey MK4).","<strong>Metronome &amp; Count-In:</strong> Enable Live's Metronome (<code>C</code>). Set Count-In to <strong>1 Bar</strong> (or <strong>2 Bars</strong> for high-BPM Drum &amp; Bass tracking at 174 BPM).","<strong>Pre-Roll Monitoring:</strong> Listen to the full count-in to internalize the metric subdivision before striking the first downbeat."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-5-2","title":"7.5.2: The Layer-by-Layer Recording Workflow","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.2: The Layer-by-Layer Recording Workflow","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  THE THREE-PASS RECORDING PROTOCOL                      |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ PASS 1: THE FOUNDATION (Kick &amp; Snare Skeleton) ]                     |\n|  • Establishes project metric clock and genre backbeat                  |\n|  • Keep timing locked to downbeats (Steps 1, 5, 9, 13)                  |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ PASS 2: THE HIGH-FREQUENCY ENGINE (Hi-Hats &amp; Shakers) ]              |\n|  • Engage MIDI Overdub (+); layer continuous 16th-note stream           |\n|  • Focus purely on dynamic velocity waves and two-finger alternation    |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ PASS 3: PERCUSSION &amp; GHOST EMBELLISHMENTS ]                          |\n|  • Layer ghost snares, rimshots, crash cymbals, and tom fills           |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-5-3","title":"7.5.3: Record Quantization: Record In vs. Post-Quantize","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.3: Record Quantization: Record In vs. Post-Quantize","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Live's top menu: <strong>Edit → Record Quantization</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                      RECORD QUANTIZATION DECISION MATRIX\n==================================================================================================\nSETTING            BEHAVIOR DURING RECORDING            BEST PRODUCTION APPLICATION\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOff (No Quantize)  Records raw human timing (exact ticks) Professional finger drummers; jazz/lo-fi\nSixteenth (1/16)   Auto-snaps hits to nearest 16th step Fast DnB tracking; dubstep kick/snare\nEighth (1/8)       Snaps hits to quarter/eighth beats   House 4-on-the-floor kick tracking\nEighth Triplet     Snaps hits to 1/8T triplet grid      Riddim / UK Garage triplet tracking\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Rule:</em> If you plan to apply custom groove templates from the Groove Pool, record with Record Quantization set to <strong>Off</strong> or <strong>1/16</strong>, then apply the groove non-destructively."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-5-4","title":"7.5.4: 16-Step Sequencing from the Launchkey MK4","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.4: 16-Step Sequencing from the Launchkey MK4","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Shift + Pad 2 (Sequencer) ] ---&gt; Pad Array Converts to 16-Step Matrix\n                                          |\n                                          +--&gt; Step Active   = Pad Backlight Solid Color\n                                          +--&gt; Step Inactive = Pad Dim / Off\n                                          +--&gt; Playhead Scan = Bright White Cursor Sweep</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Hardware Step-Sequencing Protocol:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live, select the target Drum Rack pad (e.g., Pad C1 - Kick).","On Launchkey MK4, press <code>Shift + Pad 2</code> to enter <strong>Sequencer Mode</strong>.","Tap Pad 1 and Pad 9 on the bottom row. Pads illuminate green.","Press Play. Live executes kick hits on Step 1 and Step 9.","Select Pad D1 (Snare). Tap Pad 9 (Beat 3). The Dubstep half-time skeleton is instantly sequenced entirely from hardware."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-5-5","title":"7.5.5: Capture MIDI: Rescuing Unrecorded Performances","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.5: Capture MIDI: Rescuing Unrecorded Performances","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If you jam a brilliant drum pattern on the Launchkey pads while Live is playing but forgot to press Record:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Producer Plays Live Jam on MK4 Pads ] ---&gt; Live Buffers MIDI Events in Background\n                                                    |\n                                                    v (Press \"Capture MIDI\" Button / Ctrl+Shift+C)\n[ Live Instantiates New MIDI Clip ]     &lt;--- Exact notes, timing, and tempo perfectly preserved!</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Hardware Command:</strong> Press the <strong>Capture MIDI</strong> button directly on the Launchkey MK4 transport section. Live instantly reconstructs the performance into a clean looping clip."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-5-6","title":"7.5.6: Note Repeat & Fixed Length Recording","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.6: Note Repeat & Fixed Length Recording","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Fixed Length:</strong> In Session View, configure clip recording to a fixed length (e.g., <strong>2 Bars</strong> or <strong>4 Bars</strong>). Live automatically punches out of recording at the end of the phrase and begins seamless looping playback.","<strong>Arp / Note Repeat:</strong> Holding the <code>Arp</code> button on the Launchkey MK4 enables hardware Note Repeat on pads, allowing continuous 1/16 or 1/32 snare and hi-hat rolls to be generated by holding a single pad."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l7-5-7","title":"7.5.7: Overdub vs. New Clip Iteration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"7.5.7: Overdub vs. New Clip Iteration","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>MIDI Overdub (<code>+</code> Icon / <code>O</code> Key):</strong> Adds new notes to the existing active clip without erasing previous passes.","<strong>Duplicate &amp; Iterate (<code>Ctrl + D</code>):</strong> Always duplicate the current 8-bar drum clip before tracking experimental fills, creating a safe version history (<code>Drums_v1</code>, <code>Drums_v2_Fills</code>)."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 7.5: Hardware Tracking & Sequencing Workshop","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create an empty 2-bar MIDI clip in Ableton Live on your Drum Rack track.","Engage <strong>Record Quantization: 1/16</strong>.","<strong>Pass 1:</strong> Record the Kick and Snare foundation for a Dubstep track at <code>140.00 BPM</code>.","Engage <strong>MIDI Overdub (<code>+</code>)</strong>.","<strong>Pass 2:</strong> Perform continuous eighth-note closed hi-hats live using two-finger alternation.","Switch Launchkey MK4 to <strong>Sequencer Mode</strong> (<code>Shift + Pad 2</code>). Select the Rimshot pad and sequence hits on Steps 4, 8, 12, 16 from the hardware pads.","Save the resulting clip."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What keyboard shortcut in Ableton Live 12 instantly captures an unrecorded MIDI performance?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <code>Ctrl + Shift + C</code> on Windows (<code>Cmd + Shift + C</code> on macOS).","<strong>What occurs when MIDI Overdub (<code>+</code>) is engaged during loop recording?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> New MIDI notes performed on the pads are non-destructively merged into the existing clip without erasing previously recorded note data.","<strong>How do you step-sequence a drum voice from the Launchkey MK4 hardware without using the computer mouse?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Select the target voice pad in Drum Rack, press <code>Shift + Pad 2 (Sequencer)</code> on the Launchkey, and tap the 16 physical pads to toggle 16th-note step triggers."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]}]};
